B. CAPABILITY ANALYSIS — why IOS_EXIT / MAC_EXIT are BLOCKED (not red-as-green)
================================================================================
The two `xcodebuild ... build test` steps cannot run on this runner. This is a
platform-capability gap, exactly the condition the prompt names ("If unavailable
on the runner -> STATUS=BLOCKED with API evidence"):

  1. NO XCODE. The active developer directory is the Command Line Tools, not a
     full Xcode (`xcode-select -p` -> /Library/Developer/CommandLineTools).
     `xcodebuild -version` -> "tool 'xcodebuild' requires Xcode". xcodebuild is
     the only tool that can drive the iOS-Simulator + macOS `build test` gate, so
     both steps fail before compiling a single file.
  2. NO iOS SIMULATOR SDK / RUNTIME. `xcrun --sdk iphonesimulator --show-sdk-path`
     -> "SDK 'iphonesimulator' cannot be located". The iPhone 16 destination is
     unreachable on a CLT-only host.

Even on a full-Xcode host, the FULL-APP `xcodebuild` gate is additionally blocked
by PRE-EXISTING, OUT-OF-SCOPE conditions (this prompt's allowed files are only
`ChargePlansWidget.*` + this log — fixing them is forbidden here and is the
documented job of `p4-apple/P99-0001-apple-acceptance-gate` on a macos-latest
runner; see apps/macos-pending-verifications.md):

  - KMP `Shared.xcframework` is not staged. Both app schemes run a pre-build
    Run Script (`Scripts/build-shared-xcframework.sh`) that needs the
    Kotlin/Native + Gradle toolchain (gradle is not on PATH on this host); the
    binary is itself macOS-pending (deferred to p5-hardening/H8-0001).
  - Parallel-merge symbol collisions across the 54-widget parity tree:
    `struct DashboardWidgetSize` is defined 16x and `DashboardWidgetRegistration`
    14x across TeslaSync/dashboard-widgets + Sources/DashboardWidgets, and 263
    co-located `*.Tests.swift` files import XCTest into the app target. This
    surface deliberately follows the SAME self-contained per-widget convention as
    the committed reference `WallConnectorWidget` (its allowed-files scope forbids
    extracting a shared definition); it does not introduce a new class of problem.
  - macOS app target requires a development signing identity.

CONCLUSION: IOS_EXIT and MAC_EXIT are environment/predecessor blocked, NOT caused
by this surface. The surface itself is verified GREEN below by every host-runnable
means the CLT Swift 6.3.2 toolchain affords.

